From: chasey-dev <ellenyoung0912@gmail.com>
Subject: [mt_wifi7] 069 patch 已经握住总线，就别再抢回来

The 068 run changes the picture.  With the vendor order back in place the
patch download goes through cleanly - four descriptors, the DMA index walks
from one to four, PATCH_FINISH_REQ is fetched - and the endpoint drops ten
milliseconds later, as before.  What is new is what the recovery finds when
it comes back: three good config reads in a row and

  diag: restore #0 -> 3 (cmd=0x0006 lpctl=0x00000004)

LPCTL bit 2 is OWNER_STATE_SYNC, set by the firmware itself, so this is not
noise from a shut memory window - the patch really did take the chip over.
It then holds it: the wait in the recovery gives it two seconds and it never
hands back, so the recovery takes the bus by force, and from that moment on
the MCU serves nothing: the semaphore release is fetched by the command ring
and never answered, and the RAM download ring is never touched again.

So the code that was meant to rescue the download is what ends the patch.
Leave the hand-over alone and let the side that is supposed to serve the RAM
download keep the chip: diag_keep_fw_own=1 skips both the wait and the forced
pulse, rebuilds the rings and queues the download as usual.
diag_fw_own_ms stretches the wait instead, for the case where the patch just
needs longer than two seconds to hand back on its own.

Signed-off-by: chasey-dev <ellenyoung0912@gmail.com>
---
---
--- a/wlan_hwifi/bus/mtk_pci_dma.c	2026-11-28 17:24:00.000000000 +0800
+++ b/wlan_hwifi/bus/mtk_pci_dma.c	2026-11-28 17:24:00.000000000 +0800
@@ -2468,6 +2468,35 @@
 pci_dma_diag_rx_snapshot(struct pci_trans *trans, const char *tag)
 {
 }
+
+/*
+ * 069: everything above assumes the forced hand-over is harmless, and the
+ * 068 run shows it is not.  LPCTL reads back as 0x00000004 - the patch owns
+ * the chip - for as long as the wait below gives it, and the moment the
+ * forced pulse takes the bus back the MCU stops serving commands for good:
+ * the semaphore release is fetched and never answered, the RAM download ring
+ * is never fetched at all.  So make the hand-over optional, and make the wait
+ * long enough to tell "gives it back late" from "never gives it back".
+ */
+#include <linux/module.h>
+
+static bool diag_keep_fw_own;
+module_param(diag_keep_fw_own, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(diag_keep_fw_own,
+		 "leave FW_OWN alone after the patch takes the chip over");
+
+static unsigned int diag_fw_own_ms = 2000;
+module_param(diag_fw_own_ms, uint, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(diag_fw_own_ms,
+		 "wait this long for the patch to hand the chip back");
+
+/*
+ * Same two registers the MCU side prints: CONN_ON_MISC tells whether the
+ * download state is still on, SWDEF_MODE is written by the firmware itself,
+ * so a change in either while FW_OWN is held means the patch is executing.
+ */
+#define DIAG_FW_OWN_ON_MISC	0x000E00F0
+#define DIAG_FW_OWN_SWDEF	0x009143C
 
 /*
  * Bring-up diagnostic: the endpoint drops its own PCIe configuration the
@@ -2604,31 +2634,42 @@
 		 * what a started patch does - and only force it when it never
 		 * comes, otherwise the command we queue next is never fetched.
 		 */
-		for (i = 0; i < 200; i++) {
-			if (!(bus_read(btrans, trans->lp_fw_own_reg_addr) & 0x4))
-				break;
-			msleep(10);
+		if (diag_keep_fw_own) {
+			dev_err(to_device(trans),
+				"diag: keep fw own lpctl=0x%08x on_misc=0x%08x swdef=0x%08x\n",
+				bus_read(btrans, trans->lp_fw_own_reg_addr),
+				bus_read(btrans, DIAG_FW_OWN_ON_MISC),
+				bus_read(btrans, DIAG_FW_OWN_SWDEF));
+		} else {
+			for (i = 0; i < diag_fw_own_ms / 10; i++) {
+				if (!(bus_read(btrans,
+					       trans->lp_fw_own_reg_addr) &
+				      0x4))
+					break;
+				msleep(10);
+			}
+
+			lpctl = bus_read(btrans, trans->lp_fw_own_reg_addr);
+			dev_err(to_device(trans),
+				"diag: fw release after %d ms (lpctl=0x%08x)\n",
+				(lpctl & 0x4) ? -1 : i * 10, lpctl);
+
+			if (lpctl & 0x4) {
+				if (dev->chip_drv->ctl_ops->driver_own)
+					ret = dev->chip_drv->ctl_ops->driver_own(dev,
+										 btrans,
+										 true);
+				else
+					ret = -EOPNOTSUPP;
+
+				dev_err(to_device(trans),
+					"diag: driver own ret=%d lpctl=0x%08x\n",
+					ret,
+					bus_read(btrans,
+						 trans->lp_fw_own_reg_addr));
+			}
 		}
 
-		lpctl = bus_read(btrans, trans->lp_fw_own_reg_addr);
-		dev_err(to_device(trans),
-			"diag: fw release after %d ms (lpctl=0x%08x)\n",
-			(lpctl & 0x4) ? -1 : i * 10, lpctl);
-
-		if (lpctl & 0x4) {
-			if (dev->chip_drv->ctl_ops->driver_own)
-				ret = dev->chip_drv->ctl_ops->driver_own(dev,
-									 btrans,
-									 true);
-			else
-				ret = -EOPNOTSUPP;
-
-			dev_err(to_device(trans),
-				"diag: driver own ret=%d lpctl=0x%08x\n",
-				ret,
-				bus_read(btrans, trans->lp_fw_own_reg_addr));
-		}
-
 		pci_dma_diag_rx_snapshot(trans, "pre-rebuild");
 
